-- title:
--   Finite commutative Hopf algebras over an algebraically closed field
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field and let $B$ be a commutative ring carrying the structure of a Hopf algebra over $k$ which is finite as a $k$-module (both taken in the same universe). The assertion is that there exist natural numbers $n$ and $r$ and a function $q \colon \mathrm{Fin}\,r \to \mathbb{N}$ such that, for each index $i$, one has $q_i > 1$ and $q_i = (\operatorname{ringExpChar} k)^{e}$ for some natural number $e$ — that is, each $q_i$ is a power of the characteristic exponent of $k$, equal to $1$ if $k$ has characteristic $0$ and to $p$ if $k$ has characteristic $p > 0$ — together with an isomorphism of $k$-algebras
--   $$B \;\simeq\; \prod_{j=1}^{n} \; k[X_1,\dots,X_r]/(X_1^{q_1},\dots,X_r^{q_r}),$$
--   the target being the $k$-algebra of functions on $\mathrm{Fin}\,n$ with values in the quotient of $\mathrm{MvPolynomial}\,(\mathrm{Fin}\,r)\,k$ by the ideal generated by the range of $i \mapsto X_i^{q_i}$. The isomorphism is asserted only as the non-emptiness of the type of such $k$-algebra equivalences; no Hopf-algebra compatibility is claimed, and $n$ is not asserted to be positive. Note that in characteristic $0$ the condition $q_i > 1$ forces $r = 0$, so that $B$ is then a finite product of copies of $k$.
--
--   This is the classical structure theorem for finite commutative group schemes over an algebraically closed field: the connected–étale splitting makes the étale part constant, and the connected part is the spectrum of a truncated polynomial algebra, while in characteristic $0$ Cartier's theorem makes every such group scheme étale. It is used downstream in the analysis of kernels of surjections from polynomial rings onto such Hopf algebras, via [`HopfAlgebra.exists_ker_map_localization_eq_span_of_surjective_mvPolynomial_of_isMaximal`](thm.html#HopfAlgebra.exists_ker_map_localization_eq_span_of_surjective_mvPolynomial_of_isMaximal).
